    'The unit is the royal cubit because the text measures in cubits; the metre equivalent (0.523 m) is a modern convention. The scale bar on the model gives a round number of cubits at the point of focus with its metric equivalent; Nu\'s "measures" are never converted.',
    'The places are arranged along the sun\'s path, west to east and down and up. The papyrus gives a sequence of spells, not a map.',
    'The land of the living is drawn as a translucent slab so the Duat shows beneath it: a section drawing.',
    'The ninth pylon\'s "three hundred and fifty measures" is drawn one measure to one cubit, as a round plan; the unit is not in the text, and the witnesses (Nu, the Leyden papyrus) give the girth only, never a plan.',
    'The blessed in the Field are 7 cubits tall on Budge\'s "Their length is seven cubits"; the referent of "their" is a reading.',
    'The boat of Ra is 7 cubits long on the rubric\'s ritual boat, the only boat length the text gives.',
    'Guardians and gods are silhouettes keyed to the text\'s head and implement. Faces, dress and colour are not drawn because the text does not give them.',
    'The facsimile sheets appear only as evidence crops beside the ledger, never as textures on the model.',
    'The fourteen Aats of the Papyrus of Nu are a witness layer in a band along the north of the Duat, by editorial choice; Nu gives no positions. Their paint rubrics are floor tints, not ground.',
    'Aat IV\'s "measures" are drawn one measure to one cubit for display only; the unit is not identified, and the second reading of its width (10) is outlined inside the first (230).',
    'Pylons XI to XXI of Spell 146 (Nu to XIV, Heru-em-khebit from XV; Budge 1898) are a witness layer beyond Ani\'s tenth pylon, drawn as translucent ghosts in a second row behind Ani\'s line at the model\'s height and spacing; the editions give neither, and Ani has ten.',
    'The entrance door of the Hall carries the part names of the Nu conclusion of Spell 125 as labels in both translators\' wording; the door\'s size is the model\'s, and the parts are named, not measured.',
    'The Field of the Papyrus of Nebseni (Spell 110, Budge 1898) is a witness overlay south of Ani\'s islands, by editorial choice, with invented sizes; Nebseni gives names and counts, not dimensions, and his stream of one thousand [measures] is not converted.'
